.TH CHTTPSVR_RESP_SET_STATUS 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_resp_set_status \- set the HTTP response status code
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_resp_set_status(chttpsvr_resp *resp, int status_code);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets the HTTP status code for the response; the default is 200.

The library stores status_code exactly as given and does no checking
here.  When the server sends the response, a value outside the valid
HTTP status-code range of 100 to 999 goes on the wire as 500 Internal
Server Error instead, although the stored value itself does not
change.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A status_code of 1xx (informational), 204 or 304 drops the response
body on the wire, including whatever chttpsvr_resp_write,
chttpsvr_resp_write_str, chttpsvr_resp_printf or
chttpsvr_resp_write_json has already written, because RFC 9110 6.4.1,
15.2.1 and 15.4.5 forbid all three of these codes from ever carrying a
body.  None of the three gets an automatic Content-Length header,
though a 304 carries the one that the handler set, if any.  A 1xx also
gets no automatic Date header.  See the NOTES section of
chttpsvr_resp_write(3).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_resp_set_header (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_write (3)
